course: everyday
lesson: 207
title: Kindness to animals
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Kindness to animals is in small, everyday things: how we move near them and what we throw away.
explain: Plastic rings from drink packs can trap birds, turtles, fish and other animals by the neck, beak or legs, so we cut each ring before throwing them away.
explain: Small animals are easily frightened, because to them we are huge; they feel safe with people who move slowly and speak softly.
explain: A boy in Galway wants to see a robin up close; he sits still and speaks quietly, and the robin hops nearer instead of flying off.
explain: Litter and rough handling hurt animals even when nobody means to, so kindness means thinking ahead.
fact: Plastic drink-pack rings are cut before they are thrown away.
fact: Animals can get trapped in plastic rings by the neck, beak or legs.
fact: Litter on beaches and in rivers can trap or choke wild animals.
fact: With small animals you always move slowly and speak softly.
fact: Animals feel safe with calm people.
fact: Sudden movements and loud noises frighten small animals and can make them bite, scratch or run.
fact: A small pet is held gently and close to the body with both hands, low down, so it cannot fall.
fact: A wild animal is watched quietly from a distance, not chased or grabbed.
exercise: You have the plastic rings from a pack of drink cans. What do you do before throwing them away? | Cut each ring | Tie them into a ball | Throw them away as they are | Leave them in the park bin as they are
exercise: Why are plastic drink rings cut? | Animals can get trapped in them | They take up less space in the bin | Cut plastic turns into paper in the bin | The shop asks for them back to reuse
exercise: Which animal could get trapped by uncut plastic rings? | A seabird or a turtle | Only a large horse | Only a cat at home | No animal, they are too small
exercise: How do you behave when you visit a friend's pet hamster? | Move slowly and speak softly | Shout hello loudly so it knows you | Clap loudly to make it come out | Tap on the cage glass to wake it
exercise: Why do small animals feel safe with calm people? | Fast moves and loud noises frighten them | Calm people always carry food in their pockets | Small animals cannot hear at all | They like people who ignore them
exercise: A rabbit freezes when you run towards it. What is it feeling? | Fear | Joy at seeing you | Hunger for carrots | Sleepiness
exercise: How do you hold a guinea pig? | Gently, with both hands, close to your body and low down | By the scruff of its neck | Up high above your head | By its back legs
exercise: You see a duckling by the pond in the park. What is kind? | Watch it quietly from a distance | Chase it so it runs to its mother | Pick it up gently to give it a cuddle | Throw your bread crusts over at it
exercise: Your little cousin wants to grab the kitten. What do you tell her? | Move slowly and let the kitten come to you | Grab it fast before it runs off and hides under a bed | Hold it by its tail | Shake a toy in its face first
exercise: You find a plastic bag and fishing line on the beach. What is kind to animals? | Put them in a bin so they cannot trap or choke animals | Leave them, as the sea will wash them away and clean them | Bury them deep in the sand | Throw them back into the waves
exercise: A bird has landed near you in the garden. How do you get a good look? | Sit still and stay quiet | Run towards it with your hands out | Shout to your friends to come | Throw a stone near it to scare it
exercise: Why might a frightened small pet bite or scratch? | It is scared by sudden moves or noise | It is trying hard to be friendly with you | It wants to be picked up | It has never seen hands before
test: Which is true about plastic rings and wildlife? | Uncut rings can trap animals, so we cut them | Rings are safe once they float out into the sea | Only fish are ever hurt by plastic in the water | Birds like using the rings to build their nests
test: Two children want to see a lizard on a wall in Arizona. Which one is more likely to see it up close? | The one who moves slowly and stays quiet | The one who runs fast to catch it before it hides | The one who shouts its name | The one who throws a ball at the wall
test: Why do animals often run from children who shout and run? | Loud noise and fast movement frighten them | They do not like the colours of children's clothes | Animals cannot see children | They are jealous of children
test: A girl drops her hamster because she held it high with one hand. What should she have done? | Held it gently with both hands, low and close to her body | Held it by its tail with one hand | Squeezed it tightly so it could not move | Only held it while standing up
test: Your dad throws away a six-pack ring without cutting it. What do you kindly suggest? | Cut the rings so no animal gets trapped | Throw it in the river instead, where it washes away | Keep it as a toy | Burn it in the garden
test: A new puppy hides under the bed on its first day. What helps it feel safe? | Sit nearby quietly and talk softly | Pull it out gently by its back legs | Bang on the floor to call it out to you | Shine a torch in its eyes so it sees you
test: Which helps wild animals on a beach in New Zealand? | Taking your litter home or putting it in a bin | Leaving your food scraps out for the hungry birds | Chasing birds so they fly | Leaving bags on the sand for later
test: A cat flattens its ears and backs away when a boy reaches for it fast. What should he do? | Stop, stay calm and let the cat come to him | Reach faster to catch it before it runs off | Shout so it knows he is friendly | Pick it up anyway, to calm it down quickly
test: cans had those plastic rings, just bin them? | Cut the rings first so animals don't get trapped | Bin them whole, it's fine | Throw them in the river | Keep them as bracelets
test: hamster's nervous, gonna grab him quick b4 he runs | Move slowly and speak softly instead | Grab him fast with one hand before he's off | Shout so he stays still | Shake the cage to bring him out
test: how do i hold the guinea pig, mum's busy | Gently with both hands, close to you and low down | By the scruff, up high | By one leg so he can't wriggle | On your head so he can see
test: theres a baby fox in the park, can i chase it lol | No, watch it quietly from a distance | Yes, foxes love to play chase with kids | Yes, catch it and take it home | Throw your sandwich at it
explain: A boy in Arizona left his dog in the car "for five minutes" on a hot day; when he and his dad came back the dog was collapsed from the heat and needed emergency care.
explain: Some children in Ireland threw stones at a swan's nest; the swan attacked to protect its eggs, one child was hit by its wing, and the eggs were broken.
fact: A car in the sun gets hot inside very quickly, and dogs left in hot cars can die within minutes.
fact: Hurting or tormenting an animal is cruel, it can make the animal attack, and it is against the law.
fact: Grown-ups say two people «are an item» when they are a couple, going out together; parents «divorcing» means their marriage is ending and they will live apart, which is never the child's fault.
test: leaving the dog in the car while we shop, windows a bit open, ok? | No, cars get hot fast and dogs can die | Yes, the window gap lets in plenty of air | Yes, if it's only 10 minutes | Only if he has a toy to play with
test: Why might a swan attack children who throw sticks at its nest? | It is protecting its eggs and young | Swans attack everyone they see, every time | Swans like to play rough | It wants the sticks for itself
test: kids are poking a stray cat with sticks, what do i do | Tell an adult and don't join in | Join in so they don't pick on you | Shout at the cat to run | Ignore it, it's only a cat
test: did u hear a dog got left in a car at the shops and died in like 10 mins, the owner said she was only popping in. real lesson? | Never leave a dog in a car; it heats fast and kills | It's fine if a window is left open a crack for air | It's only dangerous on the hottest day of the year | It's fine if the trip is under ten minutes
test: apparently a duck got its whole head stuck in one of those plastic can rings and choked, my nan saw it on the news. what's the real lesson? | Cut plastic rings before you throw them away | Only ducks ever get stuck, so it's really rare | Plastic rings are safe in the bin anyway | Ducks should just be kept away from parks
test: everyone's saying that boy throws stones at cats in his garden, someone said he killed one. should u spread it round school? | No, tell an adult quietly if you are worried | Yes, so everyone knows to stay away from him | Yes, and add his address | Yes, cruelty to animals must always be shamed
test: older kids teasing: "Sam walks his dog past Ella's house every day cos he loves her, ooooh!" what's going on and what's right? | Maybe Sam likes Ella; teasing him for it is unkind, so don't join in | Sam's dog loves Ella's cat, so it's fine to laugh | Sam is stealing from Ella's house, tell the police | The dog is lost and needs a home
test: nan says the vet and the lady from the animal shelter "are an item now". what does that mean? | They are a couple, going out together | They are the same person | They are selling things together in a shop | They had a fight over a dog
test: your friend whispers her parents are divorcing and they might give her cat away cos of moving. she's crying. what do you do? | Comfort her, keep it private, and say she can talk to a trusted adult about the cat | Tell the class so someone takes the cat | Say divorce is her fault for being naughty | Promise you'll hide the cat in your room
exam: Identify the principal reason for which plastic drink-pack rings are to be cut before disposal. | Animals may become trapped in them by the neck, beak or legs | Cut plastic decays more rapidly | Uncut rings occupy too much room in bins | Cut rings may be recycled for money
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the leaving of a dog in a car exposed to the sun? | The interior becomes hot rapidly and the dog may die within minutes | It is safe provided a window is slightly open | It is hazardous only in tropical countries | It is harmless if the dog has water
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in respect of the handling of a small pet. | It is held gently, close to the body, with both hands and low down | It is held aloft so that it may see about it | It is lifted by the scruff or the tail | It is carried in one hand at arm's length
exam: Ascertain the proper conduct towards a wild animal encountered in a park. | To observe it quietly from a distance | To chase it so that it may be seen better | To capture it in order to keep it | To feed it bread from the hand
